        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">One Thousand and One Nights</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 700 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Many authors, translators and scholars across West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> over many centuries; the frame story comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> Persian Hezār Afsān, and the French translator Antoine Galland added tales he heard from the Syrian writer Hanna Diyab</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mostly Arabic prose, with verse for songs, riddles and heightened emotion; tales framed within tales and divided into nights, from a few hundred in some editions to 1001 or more in others</span></div>
