    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>There is no single canonical text: tales of Indian and Persian origin were joined by Arabic stories of Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and the Egyptian collections kept growing into the 18th and 19th centuries.</li><li>Aladdin and Ali Baba, its best-known tales in the West, are in none of the original Arabic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>; Antoine Galland added them after hearing them from Hanna Diyab in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1709.</li><li>Galland's French version of 1704 made it famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its frame story, nested tales and cliffhangers have since shaped writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> to Borges and films from Méliès to Disney.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Since 1982 the International Astronomical Union has named features on Saturn's moon Enceladus after characters and places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a>'s translation of the Nights.</p></aside>
    </section>

